<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kostrzyn nad Odrą</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Küstrin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kostrzyn nad Odrą, in German Küstrin, is a town in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warta">Warta</a> joins the Oder. Margrave John of Brandenburg-Küstrin made it his capital and a powerful fortress in the 16th century, and in 1730 the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> was imprisoned there. The old town was destroyed in 1945, and the town then became Polish.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kostrzyn-nad-odra.jpg" alt="Kostrzyn nad Odrą" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kapitel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kostrzyn_nad_Odra_dron_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
